"Places stakeholder groups in contextual layers around an initiative to clarify involvement and surrounding organizational boundaries.\n\nUse when: Discover stakeholders beyond the immediate delivery team. Discuss direct participation versus broader organizational or external context.\n\nAvoid when: Stakeholders need ranking by power or a map of detailed transactions.\n\nVisual grammar: Concentric regions represent explicitly named involvement or context layers. Stakeholder labels belong within the relevant layer. Membership placement expresses context rather than reporting authority or numerical distance.\n\nBuild: Define the focal initiative and the meaning of every ring. Identify directly involved roles, surrounding functions and external groups. Check omitted affected groups and record stakeholders with multiple roles.\n\nCheck: Each ring has a clear, consistent inclusion criterion. Physical position is not interpreted as a quantitative influence score. Multiple stakeholder roles are preserved through references when needed.\n\nPitfalls: Leaving ring meanings unstated and inviting arbitrary interpretation. Assuming outer-ring stakeholders are less affected or less important.\n\nExample: A licensing change places service staff near the core, agency functions around them and applicants and regulators in external context layers.\n\nAlternatives: Stakeholder power-interest grid, Organization chart."
